      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-13143",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The elements of the given set are the natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $5n - 2 \\leq 21.$ \n\nSo, we first need to solve our inequality for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n-2 & \\le 21 \\\\ 5n & \\le 23 \\\\ n & \\le \\frac{23}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe positive integer solutions of the inequality above are $1,2,3,4.$ Therefore, the given set can be written as \n\n$$\nA = \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038440/example-13143.json",
          "file_sha256": "417f5793d876ca927d4bbab68405e8cbdd8e28a0d09f5d953bfdcf77b4a5b489"
        }
