      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-13176",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The elements of the given set are the integers that satisfy the inequality $x^2 - 9 < 0.$\n\nSo, we first need to solve our inequality for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & <0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & <9 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & <3 \\\\ -3<x & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integer solutions of the inequality above are $0,\\pm1,\\pm2.$ Therefore, the given set can be written as \n\n$$\nA = \\{ 0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2\\}.\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14038440/example-13176.json",
          "file_sha256": "db2b8a6d43c34f007d1540c27ea6d045feadfc562205ea9a133721b1dc620ef7"
